## Proof

**Setup and Definitions.** For a connected reductive group $G$ over a global function field $K$ with a chosen place $v_0$ (playing the role of "infinity"), the class number is:
$$h(G) = \#\bigl(G(K)\backslash G(\mathbb{A}_K)\,/\,G(\mathbb{A}_K^{\infty})\bigr), \qquad G(\mathbb{A}_K^{\infty}) := \prod_{v \neq v_0} G(\mathcal{O}_v).$$

We take $K = \mathbb{F}_q(x)$, the function field of $C = \mathbb{P}^1_{\mathbb{F}_q}$.

**Step 1: The class number of $\mathbf{GL}_2$ over $K$ is 1.**

The double coset space $\mathbf{GL}_2(K)\backslash \mathbf{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)\,/\,\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v)$ classifies isomorphism classes of rank-2 vector bundles on the affine curve $C \setminus \{v_0\}$. For any point $v_0$ on $\mathbb{P}^1$, the complement $C\setminus\{v_0\} \cong \mathbb{A}^1_{\mathbb{F}_q}$ has coordinate ring $\mathbb{F}_q[t]$ (a PID). Over a PID, every finitely generated projective module is free, so every rank-2 vector bundle is trivial. Hence:
$$h(\mathbf{GL}_2) = 1, \quad\text{i.e.,}\quad \mathbf{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K) = \mathbf{GL}_2(K)\cdot\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v). \tag{$\star$}$$

**Step 2: Reduction from $\mathbf{PGL}_2$ to $\mathbf{GL}_2$.**

Use the exact sequence of algebraic groups:
$$1 \to \mathbf{G}_m \xrightarrow{\;\text{scalars}\;} \mathbf{GL}_2 \to \mathbf{PGL}_2 \to 1.$$

On adelic and rational points:
- $\mathbf{PGL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K) = \mathbf{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)\,/\,\mathbb{A}_K^{\times}$,
- $\mathbf{PGL}_2(K) = \mathbf{GL}_2(K)\,/\,K^{\times}$,
- $\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{PGL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v) = \bigl(\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v)\bigr)\,/\,\widehat{\mathcal{O}}^{\times}$, where $\widehat{\mathcal{O}}^{\times} = \prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathcal{O}_v^{\times}$.

An element of $\mathbf{PGL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$ is a coset $[g]$ with $g \in \mathbf{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$, and $[g_1], [g_2]$ lie in the same $(\mathbf{PGL}_2(K), \prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{PGL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v))$-double coset if and only if:
$$g_2 = \gamma\, g_1\, k\, z, \qquad \gamma \in \mathbf{GL}_2(K),\; k \in \prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v),\; z \in \mathbb{A}_K^{\times}.$$

Therefore:
$$h(\mathbf{PGL}_2) = \#\bigl(\mathbf{GL}_2(K)\backslash \mathbf{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)\,/\,(\mathbb{A}_K^{\times}\cdot\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v))\bigr). \tag{$\star\star$}$$

**Step 3: There is exactly one double coset.**

By $(\star)$, every $g \in \mathbf{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$ decomposes as $g = \gamma_0\, k_0$ with $\gamma_0 \in \mathbf{GL}_2(K)$ and $k_0 \in \prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v)$. Since $k_0 \in \prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v) \subseteq \mathbb{A}_K^{\times}\cdot\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v)$, we can write:
$$g = \gamma_0 \cdot 1 \cdot k_0 \in \mathbf{GL}_2(K)\cdot\bigl(\mathbb{A}_K^{\times}\cdot\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v)\bigr).$$

Thus every element lies in the single double coset $\mathbf{GL}_2(K)\cdot(\mathbb{A}_K^{\times}\cdot\prod_{v\neq v_0}\mathbf{GL}_2(\mathcal{O}_v))$, so the space $(\star\star)$ has cardinality 1.

**Step 4: Verification via the Brauer group (independent check).**

The connecting map $\delta: H^1(K, \mathbf{PGL}_2) \to H^2(K, \mathbf{G}_m) = \mathrm{Br}(K)$ is injective (since $H^1(K, \mathbf{GL}_2) = 1$ by Hilbert 90 for $\mathbf{GL}_n$), with image $\mathrm{Br}(K)[2]$ (quaternion algebras). The Tate–Shafarevich set satisfies:
$$\text{Ш}(\mathbf{PGL}_2) = \ker\!\Bigl[H^1(K,\mathbf{PGL}_2) \to \textstyle\prod_v H^1(K_v,\mathbf{PGL}_2)\Bigr] \cong \ker\!\Bigl[\mathrm{Br}(K)[2] \to \textstyle\prod_v \mathrm{Br}(K_v)[2]\Bigr].$$

By the **Brauer–Hasse–Noether theorem** (valid for global function fields), the localization map $\mathrm{Br}(K) \hookrightarrow \prod_v \mathrm{Br}(K_v)$ is injective. Hence $\text{Ш}(\mathbf{PGL}_2) = 0$, confirming $h(\mathbf{PGL}_2) = 1$.

**Conclusion.** Both the direct double-coset computation and the cohomological approach yield:

$$\boxed{h(\mathbf{PGL}_{2,K}) = 1}$$

for $K = \mathbb{F}_q(x)$, independent of the choice of place at infinity (since $\mathbb{P}^1_{\mathbb{F}_q}$ has trivial Jacobian and $\mathbb{F}_q[t]$ is a PID for any affine chart).

